[{"text":"Using","anchor":"scalar-field","section":"Scalar field"},{"text":"\\[\\begin{equation} \\Gamma_R (q^2) = \\Gamma_R (m^2) + (q^2 - m^2) \\Gamma'_R (m^2) + \\mathcal{O}( (q^2 - m^2)^2) \\end{equation}\\]","anchor":"scalar-field","section":"Scalar field"},{"text":"we arrive at","anchor":"scalar-field","section":"Scalar field"},{"text":"\\[\\begin{equation} \\Gamma_R(m^2) = 0, \\quad \\Gamma_R'(m^2) = 1 \\end{equation}\\]","anchor":"scalar-field","section":"Scalar field"},{"text":"which is equivalent to","anchor":"scalar-field","section":"Scalar field"},{"text":"\\[\\begin{equation} \\tilde{\\Gamma}_R(m^2) = 0, \\quad \\tilde{\\Gamma}_R'(m^2) = 0 \\end{equation}\\]","anchor":"scalar-field","section":"Scalar field"},{"text":"because setting \\(q^2 = m^2\\) kills the \\((q^2-m^2)\\)-term.","anchor":"scalar-field","section":"Scalar field"},{"text":"The bare 2-point function for the massless vector field is defined as","anchor":"massless-vector-field","section":"Massless vector field"},{"text":"\\[\\begin{equation} \\Gamma^{\\mu \\nu} (q) = -g^{\\mu \\nu} q^2 - \\frac{1-\\xi}{\\xi} q^{\\mu} q^{\\nu} - \\Pi^{\\mu \\nu} (q) \\end{equation}\\]","anchor":"massless-vector-field","section":"Massless vector field"},{"text":"and the renormalized one reads","anchor":"massless-vector-field","section":"Massless vector field"},{"text":"\\[\\begin{equation} \\Gamma_R^{\\mu \\nu} (q) = \\Gamma^{\\mu \\nu} (q) + \\;\\text{CT}. \\end{equation}\\]","anchor":"massless-vector-field","section":"Massless vector field"},{"text":"For convenience we also introduce","anchor":"massless-vector-field","section":"Massless vector field"},{"text":"\\[\\begin{equation} \\tilde{\\Gamma}^{\\mu \\nu}_R(q) = - \\Pi^{\\mu \\nu} (q) + \\;\\text{CT} \\end{equation}\\]","anchor":"massless-vector-field","section":"Massless vector field"},{"text":"which corresponds to what one actually calculates when considering the sum of a bare amplitude and the corresponding counter-term.","anchor":"massless-vector-field","section":"Massless vector field"},{"text":"The renormalization condition reads,","anchor":"massless-vector-field","section":"Massless vector field"},{"text":"\\[\\begin{equation} \\lim_{q^2 \\to 0} \\left [ \\frac{\\Gamma_R^{\\mu \\nu} (q) \\varepsilon(q)_\\nu}{q^2} = - \\varepsilon_{\\mu}(q) \\right ] \\end{equation}\\]","anchor":"massless-vector-field","section":"Massless vector field"},{"text":"where the minus sign comes from the fact that \\varepsilon^{\\ast \\mu} \\varepsilon_\\mu = -1 and \\(\\varepsilon^\\mu q_\\mu =0\\) for the projection onto the physical degrees of freedom of an on-shell massless vector boson.","anchor":"massless-vector-field","section":"Massless vector field"},{"text":"Multiplying both sides of the equality by q^2 we get","anchor":"massless-vector-field","section":"Massless vector field"},{"text":"\\[\\begin{equation} \\Gamma_R^{\\mu \\nu} (q^2) \\varepsilon_\\nu (q) = - q^2 \\varepsilon^{\\mu}(q) \\overset{q^2 \\to 0}{=} 0 \\Rightarrow \\Gamma_R^{\\mu \\nu} (q^2) \\varepsilon_\\nu (q) \\biggl|_{q^2=0} = 0, \\end{equation}\\]","anchor":"massless-vector-field","section":"Massless vector field"},{"text":"which implies that the physical particle is massless and that the renormalized particle propagator has residue 1.","anchor":"massless-vector-field","section":"Massless vector field"},{"text":"It is always possible to decompose this into transverse and longitudinal parts","anchor":"massless-vector-field","section":"Massless vector field"},{"text":"\\[\\begin{equation} \\Gamma_R^{\\mu \\nu} (q) = \\left ( g^{\\mu \\nu} - \\frac{q^\\mu q^\\nu}{q^2} \\right ) \\Gamma_{R,T} (q^2) + \\frac{q^\\mu q^\\nu}{q^2} \\Gamma_{R,L} (q^2). \\end{equation}\\]","anchor":"massless-vector-field","section":"Massless vector field"},{"text":"Plugging this into the renormalization condition and using that \\varepsilon^\\mu q_\\mu = 0, we end up with","anchor":"massless-vector-field","section":"Massless vector field"},{"text":"\\[\\begin{equation} \\lim_{q^2 \\to 0} \\left [ \\frac{\\varepsilon_{\\mu}(q) \\Gamma_{R,T} (q)}{q^2} = - \\varepsilon_{\\mu}(q) \\right ] \\end{equation}\\]","anchor":"massless-vector-field","section":"Massless vector field"},{"text":"which implies","anchor":"massless-vector-field","section":"Massless vector field"},{"text":"\\[\\begin{equation} \\lim_{q^2 \\to 0} \\left [ \\frac{\\Gamma_{R,T} (q^2)}{q^2} \\right ] = - 1 \\Leftrightarrow \\frac{\\partial \\Gamma_{R,T} (q^2) }{\\partial q^2} \\biggl |_{q^2=0} = - 1 \\end{equation}\\]","anchor":"massless-vector-field","section":"Massless vector field"},{"text":"and is equivalent to","anchor":"massless-vector-field","section":"Massless vector field"},{"text":"\\[\\begin{equation} \\frac{\\partial \\tilde{\\Gamma}_{R,T} (q^2) }{\\partial q^2} \\biggl |_{q^2=0} = 0 \\end{equation}\\]","anchor":"massless-vector-field","section":"Massless vector field"},{"text":"Notice that if our \\(\\Gamma_R^{\\mu \\nu}\\) does not happen to have a transverse structure, then most likely something went wrong in the calculation, since the longitudinal part is not supposed to receive higher-order corrections!","anchor":"massless-vector-field","section":"Massless vector field"},{"text":"In practice, we will of course use projectors to extract the transverse and longitudinal components \\begin{align*} P^{\\mu \\nu}_T &= \\frac{1}{D-1} \\left ( g^{\\mu \\nu} - \\frac{q^\\mu q^\\nu}{q^2} \\right ), \\\\ P^{\\mu \\nu}_L &= \\frac{q^\\mu q^\\nu}{q^2}. \\end{align*}","anchor":"massless-vector-field","section":"Massless vector field"},{"text":"In the special case of the QED photon things can be further simplified by making use of the Ward identity for the renormalized propagator as Green function","anchor":"massless-vector-field","section":"Massless vector field"},{"text":"\\[\\begin{equation} -\\frac{1}{Z_\\xi} \\frac{1}{\\xi} q^2 q_\\mu Z_A \\Gamma_R^{\\mu \\nu} (q) = i q^\\nu, \\end{equation}\\]","anchor":"massless-vector-field","section":"Massless vector field"}]